  "metadata": {
    "title": "Symmetry-Stratified Identifiability of Strongly Tree-Child Level-2 Phylogenetic Networks: A Unified Theory for JC, K2P, and K3P",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22395326",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-05",
    "description": "<p>The classical four-state group-based DNA models form three symmetry strata on G = Z₂ &times; Z₂: Jukes&ndash;Cantor (JC), Kimura 2-parameter (K2P), and Kimura 3-parameter (K3P) tie the three nonzero Fourier characters in one, two, and three blocks, respectively. Using exact version-bound results from three companion classifications, this work gives a unified theorem for leaf-labelled binary standard semi-directed strongly tree-child networks of level at most two. On the strict principal positive-eigenvalue domain, and separately in strict edgewise continuous time, a source-relative regular full-dimensional containment from N to N&prime; on the same finite labelled leaf set exists exactly when the networks differ only by coherent ordinary-triangle redirection. The same condition characterizes a common regular germ full-dimensional in both images. Consequently, under any one known fixed model, a generic exact complete leaf site-pattern distribution identifies the same triangle-redirection quotient among networks in this class on the same leaf set.</p>\n<p>The synthesis also proves uniform three-leaf triangle geometry. With r &isin; {1,2,3} character-tying blocks, the normalized ambient dimension is a_r = r(r+7)/2, the Jacobian rank is d_r = 5r&minus;1, the regular local fibre dimension under the standard six-edge triangle parameterization is r+2, and the ambient codimension is binomial(r&minus;1,2). It interprets the K3P H₁₄ conormal through the sign representation, proves that full-dimensional containment cannot point from a richer classical model to a poorer one, establishes monotone target-model refinement for triangle-equivalent networks, and classifies all 144 ordered cross-model relations among the labelled three-leaf tree and three ordinary-sunlet orientations. Strong tree-childness is shown to be a sharp class-level boundary through model-specific weak-class examples.</p>\n<p>The reproducibility package contains the article, technical supplement, source, four exact bounded replays, deterministic build files, verification manifests, dependency bindings, and provenance. The replays check the synthesis-specific finite and symbolic calculations; the imported global JC, K2P, K3P, and tree&ndash;theta results remain bound to their separate DOI records.</p>\n<p>The paper does not claim equality of complete stochastic images, boundary coverage, recovery under an unknown substitution model, bounded finite-sample sufficiency, complete numerical-parameter identification, all binary level-2 networks, or level-3 results.</p>\n<p>Preprint; not peer reviewed by a journal. Generative-AI assistance and its verification workflow are disclosed in the article. No specific funding supported this work. The author declares no competing interests.</p>",
